WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.800
I am very happy today, because we have&nbsp;
an incredible paper here. Get this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:11.560
it helps us transform into virtual&nbsp;
characters and transfer our movements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:18.640
to them. Wow. But it gets better - we&nbsp;
can even control virtual characters with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:25.600
different morphology than our bodies. We&nbsp;
can be a tiny little mouse, or a dinosaur.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:32.760
And wait, it gets even better. From&nbsp;
a three-point input. Not full body.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.360
This is not motion capture where&nbsp;
an entire body suit is used and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:43.680
we know the movement of every body part.&nbsp;
No sir! What we know here is so much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:50.800
challenging - just the movement of the VR&nbsp;
headset and two controllers. Nothing else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:58.960
That is really tough. And to me, it sounds&nbsp;
absolutely impossible. So, is it impossible?

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.400
Well, almost! Let’s see if this paper&nbsp;
can pull it off. Dear Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.640
this is Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:13.720
First, for a training step, it uses motion&nbsp;
capture data to learn which human joints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:21.400
match the virtual character's joints for training.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, this does not work great, but wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:29.360
don’t despair. Not all is lost. Why is that? Well,&nbsp;
we can now create a reinforcement learning agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:38.200
which can lean on this jerky animation, and learn&nbsp;
to control it properly. Here. Whoa! This looks so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:44.000
much better. Loving it. And note that at this&nbsp;
step, we can throw away all the motion capture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.840
data and we only need the headset and two&nbsp;
controllers. So far so good. So, are we done?

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.520
Well, we are not done yet.&nbsp;
Not even close. We also need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.440
a little trickery for dealing with&nbsp;
the head orientation, or otherwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:08.160
we will look like this. And it gets even worse.&nbsp;
Due to the heavy head of the mouse character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:17.400
even this can happen. Ouch. But, after this&nbsp;
additional step, look. Mmm! Much better.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.000
Additionally, to add more realism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:27.360
we can even decide how we wish to control&nbsp;
the tail of the dinosaur as well. Loving it.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:35.400
So, are we done? Well, we are still not done yet.&nbsp;
The paper also proposes enriching the training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:43.520
data with additional information for the AI to&nbsp;
learn, and then throwing this data away. But, come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:51.480
on, if we don’t do that, what’s the worst that&nbsp;
can happen? Well, this. This can happen. Ouch.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:58.240
But, putting this all together, hold on to your&nbsp;
papers Fellow Scholars, and look! It finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:05.680
works! Now, we have an absolutely miraculous&nbsp;
algorithm that can perform these movements across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:12.240
different morphologies. With different character&nbsp;
types. It does it in an energy efficient way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:20.120
so that we get relatively elegant, smooth&nbsp;
movements, and not those jerky ones. Excellent.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:25.560
But, wait a second…are you thinking&nbsp;
what I am thinking? Remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:32.320
it has information from the headset and&nbsp;
the controllers. Does that mean that? Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:38.280
that’s exactly what it means. It has&nbsp;
no lower body information whatsoever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:46.200
and yet, it can still reconstruct all these&nbsp;
movements really well. Wow, that is crazy.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.680
And it gets better. Larger and&nbsp;
smaller characters will be moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:55.120
around differently. You just&nbsp;
walk around in your own pace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:02.280
but for them, depending on their size, more&nbsp;
or maybe fewer tippy taps are required.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:09.400
And advanced movements are also possible.&nbsp;
Staggering, balancing, you name it. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:16.240
for smaller, more agile characters, that is. And&nbsp;
we can even try our dancing moves. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:22.960
they are perhaps a little inept, but in&nbsp;
a very cute way. And not just movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:27.560
but acting can be done. So good! Loving it.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.200
And yes, it gets even better. If that is at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.920
all possible. I have found three more&nbsp;
mind blowing things about this paper.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:42.920
One, it has been trained on just one&nbsp;
machine. With one consumer graphics card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:49.320
and not even a high-end one. The training&nbsp;
took 24 hours, and this step only has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:55.600
be done once, and then, we can use it&nbsp;
in real time for as long as we wish.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:03.800
Two, so how much training data is required&nbsp;
for this? Get this: only 4 hours. It looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:10.840
at 4 hours of footage from a bunch of humans, and&nbsp;
then, it generalizes the movement to new people.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:17.800
And, three. What you are about to see is the&nbsp;
reason Two Minute Papers exists. Look. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:25.400
paper has been seen by less than a 100 people.&nbsp;
Such an amazing work, and I am worried that if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.280
don’t talk about it here, almost no one will talk&nbsp;
about it. So if you Fellow Scholars would help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:39.280
me spread the word of these amazing AI / computer&nbsp;
graphics papers, that would be amazing. Thank you.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.680
Now don’t forget, this technique&nbsp;
is still not completely automatic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:49.440
I noted earlier that some of the joints of&nbsp;
the human are mapped to the new character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:56.120
so you can’t just drop in a completely new&nbsp;
character without some additional work. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:02.360
in return, vastly different morphologies work.&nbsp;
I would estimate that not a great deal of work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:09.000
has to be done per character, but it still&nbsp;
requires a bit of work an expertise. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:15.440
bet that just one more paper down the line,&nbsp;
and we will be able to do this automatically.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:21.640
Just imagine a world where we can all become&nbsp;
virtual characters, where we can play together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:27.440
and the way we move is translated by an&nbsp;
algorithm to feel like we are really any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:34.000
creature we want. Even if they have a completely&nbsp;
different morphology, big head, small head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:41.800
floppy ears, or a tail, anything you can imagine.&nbsp;
And I am absolutely blown away by the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:50.160
it may not even need to track our lower body for&nbsp;
that. Only the three-point input. Head and hands.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.160
And don’t forget, the differences&nbsp;
between these models are huge. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.160 --> 00:07:02.440
are vast variations in weight, 25x difference&nbsp;
between the lightest and heaviest character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:09.360
tail joints may or may not exist, and even if&nbsp;
they do, there is a different amount of joints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:15.200
same with ear joints, and the amount of&nbsp;
torque they can produce is also subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:21.080
to a great deal of variation. And having a&nbsp;
modern algorithm that can translate all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:29.000
our motions to these characters is absolutely&nbsp;
incredible. Wow. What a time to be alive!

